"""Write bounded lead reports and the exact sealed-queue result index."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv,json
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
Q=list(csv.DictReader((B/'queue.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf8')))
assert [x['sample_id'] for x in Q]==[f'V{i:03}' for i in range(1,11)]
cases={
'V001':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','S1 selected decline begins at a data gap and recovers as a long ramp. S63 has a dip followed by a larger positive overshoot twice, separated by only 13.5 d. The S94 BTJD 3874.95 trough occurs simultaneously in V008 (another camera/CCD) and V009 (the same C3CCD4), and S96 broad trough repeats in V009 on C3CCD3. These are positive shared detector/spacecraft signatures, not a target-specific eclipse train.','No defensible same-kind orbital clock from the selected dips; the raw q0 light curve also has sector-scale slopes and steps. KSPSAP S1 suppresses the selected 1340 feature but uses the same photons.','The selected troughs are rejected; independent target variability is not excluded.','Check target-centered valid difference pixels if an independent candidate emerges.'),
'V002':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','All nine raw sectors show a stable, very high-amplitude asymmetric waveform recurring about every 1.26 d, with trough-to-crest excursions approaching 50%. The many flagged minima are successive cycles of that waveform, not separate Gaia-22.37-d eclipse occasions. Early KSPSAP also retains large but altered modulation.','A Gaia G=8.1194 neighbor lies 8.706 arcsec (0.417 TESS pixel) from the G=11.2669 target, about 18.15 times brighter in Gaia G. The target contributes at most about 5.2% of the two-star G light, so total disappearance of the target cannot produce a 30–50% blended loss under comparable TESS/G throughput. The S17 difference centroid is closer to that neighbor (0.275 pixel) than the target (0.375), though the two are unresolved; exact variable-source ownership remains open.','Refutes attribution of the giant short-period trough train to the selected Gaia target under ordinary throughput, while preserving the blended source as an interesting variable.','Resolve the variable source and classify the short-period waveform.'),
'V003':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','S5/S6 SAP is dominated by short oscillations and scattered minima; S32 has a denser oscillatory train. Selected 1443.36, 1486.01 and 2192.61 windows have only about 0.5, -0.3 and 0.9 ppt locally normalized core loss, compared with the variable background. No consistent isolated dip morphology recurs at the Gaia 21.145-d period.','The flagged windows do not support a stable same-kind eclipse sequence; alternate KSPSAP varies at comparable small amplitude in old sectors.','Noise and oscillations limit shallow-event sensitivity; this is a refutation of the selected screening pattern, not proof of no binary.','Use higher-resolution target-centered pixels if a specific new ephemeris is proposed.'),
'V004':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','S99 selected 4053 and 4067 troughs begin at cadence gaps/steps and recover along a changing baseline. The S99 4053 feature coincides with an abrupt SAP-X centroid displacement of roughly 0.055–0.10 pixel, with Y relaxation following flux recovery. S101 4107 event likewise starts at a break; 4119 is a broad slope into a gap. S64 features at 3054/3061 are weak and gap-sensitive.','S99 4053/4067 spacing and S101 4107/4119 spacing do not define one stable same-kind clock, and Gaia 47.626 d cannot rescue gap-bound features. The gap plus centroid step is affirmative detector evidence for the selected S99 feature.','A different real event in the target is not excluded by rejecting these detector windows.','Inspect independent target-centered pixel extraction only if a clean raw repeat is found.'),
'V005':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','Thirty-two raw sectors show broad, smooth large-amplitude waves and occasional discontinuities. S62/S63/S64 apparent 27-d detector dips lie on the descending sides of waves; S90 3759 is another broad descent. They lack a discrete, two-sided eclipse core. This may be orbital or stellar modulation near the Gaia 27.282-d scale, but it does not verify eclipses.','Local trapezoid fits turn curvature into fitted depths (up to tens of ppt) and cannot time an eclipse on this baseline.','Photometric modulation itself may be astrophysical; its mechanism and source are not determined here.','Phase-fold and model the smooth variability separately if scientifically useful.'),
'V006':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','Thirty-one sectors contain persistent short-period undulating variability. Seventy flagged local minima are portions of this oscillatory train, not rare isolated eclipses; the S87 3668.85 local normalized core is actually 1.5 ppt above the surrounding ±1.4-d median.','No selected same-kind sequence tied to Gaia 22.124 d survives raw-context review. Automated local fits assign 3–8 ppt depths to pieces of the oscillation and should not be used as eclipse epochs.','A low-amplitude eclipse hidden beneath the variability is not excluded.','Search residuals after a justified variability model only if a specific repeat is proposed.'),
'V007':('PLAUSIBLE','COMPLETE','Seven q0 SAP occasions at BTJD 1546.413, 1590.499, 2317.923, 3023.369, 3750.813, 3772.869 and 4081.434 have a repeatable broad brightening-then-trough profile. Assigned cycle numbers 0,2,35,67,100,101,115 give a 22.04395-d photometric spacing with residuals within 0.03 d. The S90 P/2, P/3 and P/4 predicted windows have continuous q0 core coverage and no comparable trough. Independent target-centered TESSCut 3x3-minus-corner-sky extraction reproduces 4.51 and 4.40 ppt S37/S90 core deficits with full local core coverage and positive baselines.','Gaia SB1 period is 22.03927±0.00799 d. Its source-specific B conjunction is 0.303–0.657 d before the observed troughs over S37–S90, within fixed-cycle linear covariance allowances of 0.603–1.127 d; A conjunction is about 6 d off. Two independent S37/S90 difference peaks lie within 0.060/0.200 pixel of valid target flux. The nearest potentially capable Gaia neighbor is G16.85 at 14.99 arcsec, with G-band flux ratio 0.00272, below the roughly 4–5 ppt selected trough even for total disappearance.','This is a strong target-centered recurring orbital photometric signal, but raw and alternative curves show a smooth rounded trough after a broad bright hump, without separately resolved ingress, egress or occultation core. Heartbeat-only variability is materially viable, so an eclipse is not established. KSPSAP suppresses early features; Gaia uncertainties are fixed-cycle linear approximations and bandpass/throughput affect neighbor flux bounds.','Obtain multicolor or high-cadence photometry, or a physical heartbeat model with an additional eclipse component, before calling this an eclipsing system.'),
'V008':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','Forty-three sectors show long ramps, steps and excursions. The S94 3874.9 trough is concurrent, to the cadence, with V001 on C3CCD4 and V009 on C3CCD4 although this target is on C4CCD1. S5 and S27 flags are slope/gap related; no target-specific same-kind train is established.','The S94 cross-camera coincidence is positive evidence of spacecraft behavior. The large number of sector flags is not recurrence proof.','Other variability on this target remains unclassified.','If a different candidate event is found, obtain valid pixel-level source attribution.'),
'V009':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','S94 3874.95 has a broad raw trough and post-event step at exactly the V001 C3CCD4 and V008 C4CCD1 event time. S96 3926.4 broad decline occurs with V001 on the same C3CCD3, followed by a gap and reset. Earlier sectors lack a comparable stable same-kind event.','The 51.46-d S94/S96 separation has many possible integer-cycle aliases; coincident multi-target and gap behavior provides affirmative artifact evidence rather than a measured period.','Rejects these screened troughs as target-specific eclipses, while not ruling out unrelated binarity.','Use target-centered difference pixels if a separate clean dip appears.'),
'V010':('REFUTED','COMPLETE','Forty-three raw sectors show rapidly varying oscillatory waves with changing amplitude and phase, plus slower drifts. Ninety-two flagged minima and the S26/S58/S85 strongest examples are pieces of this dense waveform, without an isolated same-kind eclipse core or a unique Gaia-38.238-d clock.','Trapezoid fits can assign 10–20 ppt depths to local waveform troughs but do not establish eclipse times.','Variability could obscure a separate shallow eclipse; none is demonstrated by the selected pattern.','Analyze the oscillation spectrum or residuals only for a separately motivated ephemeris.')
}
rows=[]
for q in Q:
 sid=q['sample_id'];tic=q['tic'];verdict,completion,raw,timing,lim,nxt=cases[sid]
 period=''
 epoch=''
 depth=''
 report=f'{tic}/REPORT.md'
 lines=[f'# {sid} TIC {tic}: {verdict}', '',f'Selected stratum: {q["sample_stratum"]}. Bounded review: {completion}. Catalogue novelty: UNKNOWN pending exact queue receipts.','', '## Raw observations and alternative reduction','',raw,'','## Timing, attribution and geometry','',timing,'','## Limits and next check','',lim+' Next: '+nxt,'','## Evidence','',f'Original bulk scan: `../../../binary-exploration-2026-11-04/bulk-results/{tic}.json`; exact SHA256 of every cached FITS used appears in `../deciding-windows.json` and in the original bulk JSON. Source TIC identities were checked in FITS headers. All-sector q0 SAP: `all-sectors.png`, `screen.json`; selected raw and KSPSAP comparisons: `decide-*.png` and `../deciding-windows.json`; provisional flagged-window fits: `fitted-windows.json`. S94/S96 comparison: `../cross-target.json` and plots; V007 clock and actual cadence coverage: `../v007-clock.json`; Gaia source-specific conjunction: `../v007-gaia.json`; independent helper WCS, pixel difference, and aperture receipts: `../helper-*.json`, plots and helper audit. Detector window centers are predictions, and KSPSAP/TESSCut use the same observing photons.','','No catalogue nonmatch or new-system claim is made from this physical review.']
 (B/tic/'REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n',encoding='utf8')
 rows.append({'sample_id':sid,'tic':tic,'sample_stratum':q['sample_stratum'],'physical_verdict':verdict,'full_review_completion':completion,'novelty_status':'UNKNOWN','period_days':period,'period_error_days':'','primary_epoch_btjd':epoch,'depth_ppt':depth,'limitations':lim,'next_check':nxt,'report':report})
fields=['sample_id','tic','sample_stratum','physical_verdict','full_review_completion','novelty_status','period_days','period_error_days','primary_epoch_btjd','depth_ppt','limitations','next_check','report']
with (B/'RESULTS.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf8') as f:
 w=csv.DictWriter(f,fields);w.writeheader();w.writerows(rows)
